leading the charge are the public health  officers Teresa Tam is the federal  officer and each province and territory  has their own they never seek the  limelight now though they are some of  the most famous and beloved leaders in  the country their daily briefings are  where canadians turn to get the facts  and learn about the science BC's public  health officer dr. Bonnie Henry is so  beloved she's inspired a fan club  t-shirts street art and musical tributes  she's also keenly aware that this virus  came out of nowhere and that makes it  very hard to predict where it's going  dr. Henry I know you are so incredibly  busy thank you for taking the time to  talk to us you know I want to start by  asking when we watched what happened in  China and saw what this virus is capable  of back in January how it overwhelmed  the hospitals that it killed even health  care workers entire cities were put  under lockdown did you think what was  happening there could happen here do you  have a forgive me but a kind of holy  crap moment absolutely and I think you  know when talking about it with with my  colleagues to the media to the public at  the time we were holding out hope that  China would be able to contain this but  it became apparent not long after I mean  I knew we would undoubtedly have cases  in in Canada and in BC just given the  travel patterns that we knew around the  world I don't think I was prepared for  how severe it was going to be in other  countries particularly when it hit  Europe and many of us I think around the  world when we saw what was happening in  Italy was really shocking these were all  the things that we had talked about in  preparing for pandemics for a long time  but I never really thought that I would  actually have to do them but I think you  know here in Canada we we had the luxury  of time to prepare us and to help us  understand what things need it to be  done you have so much experience with  the SARS outbreak in Toronto where you  helped lead the response and the Ebola  outbreak in Uganda  those were life and death challenges -  how does what you're doing now compared  to that you know I think every pandemic  is different every outbreak is different  all of them have their challenges this  one certainly is unprecedented in the  impacts that it's had around the world  and you know as you say I've been  involved with many very serious very  tragic outbreaks and this one just the  the whole societal impacts of it have  been immense you have such a fine  balance to keep as a public health  officer informing people but not  panicking people deciding when an asked  becomes an order it's critical that you  keep the public on your side throughout  this how much does that weigh on you a  lot of it weighs on me it weighs on me  you know we want to put in enough  restrictions to do what we need to do to  contain this virus right now we know  there's there's no treatment there's no  prevention if you've been exposed we  just need to make sure that we can care  for you appropriately and that we do  everything we can so you don't pass it  on to somebody else but every single  measure that we take that unintended  consequences the numbers of people who  have had their lives disrupted whether  it's from kovat 19 and the tragedies in  our long-term care homes or whether it's  people who have had their surgeries  cancelled or people who are unemployed  because of the the huge economic fallout  I think that you know the everything  about this has been so challenging and  that the sort of balance of trying to  get just enough restriction to do what  we need to do but not too much and it it  haunts me a little bit because you can't  ever get it just right though there is  widespread transmission in some areas of  the world we are not in that position  yet I know you are a doctor dr. Henry  not a TV personality your briefings have  happened almost every day though for 12  weeks now you've become must-see TV and  British Columbia  P  or drawing pictures of you and murals of  you I'm sure they're sending you things  and they're writing songs about you  that's a barbershop quartets in the  Naima they love you it's it's pretty  remarkable isn't it can you see me  blushing that was beautiful certainly  have amazing voices I guess what I'm  asking is what do you think it says  about how the public is looking to  people like you to public health  officers and to experts and to science  to lead us out of this well I am  heartened that people are looking to  experts into science particularly in  Canada and you know I've said this many  times what I do is I'm the face and  sometimes the voice of a lot of people  and a lot of people who are very  committed and passionate and doing the  work on the ground and we are a team and  we are a team across the country we know  each other well we talk all the time  sometimes daily these days but it is a  privilege and very humbling for me to  represent the work that so many people  are doing to try and keep us safe in our  communities across this country the  million-dollar question is when this  will end now I know there's no precise  answer to that I'm not gonna ask you  that but what about an exit strategy a  plan for a ladder if you like that we  can start climbing to get out of this  you must be working on that absolutely  and in the same way that we were  watching China we were watching Italy  we're watching Singapore Hong Kong  Germany we're now watching them and  seeing where you know in some ways again  we have the luxury of everybody else  going before us right now of course we  are still very much in the middle of  things in the United States and across  Canada but we are seeing what measures  others are doing and how it works so  clearly watching Luhan very closely  where they've just relisted the  restrictions quite a lot  and we have the luxury of some modeling  we have some really brilliant minds that  have been looking at this looking at  what we might think of over time and yes  we are planning from the very first day  we put the first measures in place my  goal my aim and our direction to people  was all right what do we need to know  that we're in a place where we can stop  doing some of these things I think the  reality is that some things are going to  change for a long time maybe 18 months  maybe years things around distancing  things around cleaning our hands  regularly the things that do protect us  that that safe distance between us until  we have a herd immunity or community  immunity whether that's because people  have been infected or hopefully because  we have a vaccine that prevents us from  getting sick with this virus we're going  to have to do some things for quite some  time when is it time dr. Henry to tell  the public what that strategy is how it  will work when some of the restrictions  can be lifted and how that will happen  none of us are in the place right now  where we can consider how we're going to  lift restrictions that doesn't mean we  we aren't thinking about it and we need  to think about it but we need to be  realistic about the fact that we have a  few more weeks before we can start  actually implementing these things and  we'll talk more about what that looks  like in the near future but right now it  has to be done in a very thoughtful way  and a very a way that makes sure that we  have those surveillance pieces in place  so we know if we're starting to get more  cases again that might lead to outbreaks  and overwhelm our health system so it's  going to be a fine balance for a while  you know we sometimes talk about the  cases in the deaths as if they are  statistics they are of course and we all  know they're individual people you are  making life-and-death decisions there  must be times when that really hits you  absolutely and you know it's the thing  that keeps me up awake at night more  than anything is you know have we missed  things what have we done enough in this  area or that area you know that the  tragedies of you know recently we've had  people who've died from this at home  what can we do differently how can we  support people and the other part of it  that's so important is these measures  these unprecedented measures we've put  in place across the world you know the  impact on children the impact on people  who can't who haven't been able to get  their their surgery that they need it's  been a it's it's very challenging to to  reconcile all of those and of course  everybody who who dies from this who's  affected by this their family their  caregivers their community but I also  think you know that's what that is why  people are willing and able and are  doing what we've asked them to do around  social distancing around keeping their  in keeping that safe distance for people  staying close to their family working  from home because we know that if we get  sick with this it is our loved ones our  family in our close community that we're  going to pass it to so all of us are in  this together  and I think you know if that's the  impetus so many of us have to make sure  we're doing the right things
 

